AB  - Data Privacy and Cloud Security are two inseparable components of contemporary digital infrastructure that allow sensitive data stored or processed in a cloud-based environment to be protected against unauthorized access, abuse, or loss. The purpose of the paper is to synthetically examine the research trends, collaboration networks, and thematic developments in the world in terms of access control and encryption methods through the method of scientometrics. Although there is a lot of literature on cryptography and secure cloud frameworks, the existing literature is still divided among various fields, with no coherent knowledge of the latest trends, interdisciplinary connections, and new security models. To fill this gap, bibliometric data from the Scopus database (20202025) were examined using VOSviewer, CiteSpace, Excel, and OriginPro. The analysis demonstrates that 694 publications are in 165 sources with an annual growth rate of 36.26, highlighting the fact that cloud security research is increasing rapidly. India and China became the most productive participants, and major topics such as blockchain-powered access control, hybrid encryption, and AI-powered privacy frameworks were introduced. The analysis of keywords and citations revealed research clusters in federated learning, post-quantum cryptography, and decentralized security models. The findings provide a thorough mapping of the intellectual organization of research on cloud security and point to further directions of explainable, energy-efficient, and regulation-consistent encryption systems for reliable cloud ecosystems. Β© 2026 IEEE.
