AB  - The rapid expansion of IoT-based smart grids demands scalable, energy-efficient, and secure solutions, particularly against emerging quantum computing threats. Traditional blockchain-based systems face limitations such as high processing demands and vulnerability to quantum decryption algorithms like Shor and Grover. To address these issues, this study proposes a **Quantum-Resistant Hybrid Blockchain (QR-HB)** model that integrates NTRU lattice-based cryptography, Proof-of-Authentication (PoA) consensus, homomorphic encryption, and federated learning-guided optimization. The NTRU-based cryptographic layer ensures resistance to quantum attacks, while the PoA consensus reduces centralization risks associated with Proof-of-Stake (PoS) and significantly lowers energy consumption compared to Proof-of-Work (PoW). Homomorphic encryption enables secure computations on encrypted data, maintaining privacy during energy transactions. Additionally, federated learning enhances real-time anomaly detection, fraud prevention, and adaptive energy management across decentralized nodes. Experimental evaluations reveal that QR-HB consumes approximately 67 % less energy than PoW and operates up to 40 times more efficiently than PoS, while maintaining high throughput and minimal latency. Designed for next-generation smart grids with integrated energy storage, QR-HB provides a robust, quantum-secure, and scalable blockchain framework capable of sustaining future decentralized energy ecosystems with strong cybersecurity and operational resilience. Β© 2026
