AB  - The rapid advancement of quantum computing threatens the security of classical public-key cryptosystems widely used for secure communications in Internet of Things (IoT) and electric vehicle (EV) battery swapping networks. Existing methods, including traditional cryptography and some early post-quantum algorithms, often struggle with high latency, excessive energy consumption, and limited suitability for resource-constrained IoT devices, hindering their practical deployment. This paper proposes PostQ-SwapNet, a novel hybrid framework designed to secure IoT-based EV battery swapping stations against both classical and quantum cyber threats. The system integrates CRYSTALS-Kyber for quantum-resistant key exchange, Dilithium digital signatures for mutual authentication, AES-128 encryption for data confidentiality, and a BiLSTM-based anomaly detection module for real-time threat identification. Additionally, it leverages a private blockchain with smart contracts to ensure transaction immutability and traceability. The objective is to deliver a secure, efficient, and scalable solution compatible with current IoT hardware constraints. This framework reduces latency to 1 2 0 ~ m s, energy use to 1 5 ~ m J and memory use to 2 5 6 ~ K B and still maintains a strong level of security. Python and TensorFlow were used for coding the AI side of the project. The results affirm that PostQ-SwapNet effectively balances security and performance, making it a practical solution for future-proofing EV energy networks against quantum and conventional cyberattacks. Β© 2025 IEEE.
