AB  - The Intelligent Manufacturing of Things (IMoT) has brought significant advancements to manufacturing, with devices, sensors, and applications that enhance production system care. However, this ecosystem is highly vulnerable to cyberattacks, which can compromise production system safety and expose sensitive industrial information. Intrusion Detection and Prevention Systems (IDPS) facilitate the prevention and detection of such threats, but their effectiveness in IMoT environments presents significant challenges. Limited processing resources, interoperability issues, and a high rate of false positives make real-world deployment difficult. Recent cyberattacks on remote factory operations services and factories highlight the urgent need for adaptive security solutions. Artificial intelligence (AI)-based IDPS, blockchain-based security, and cloud-edge architecture offer promising solutions, but integrating them into existing manufacturing infrastructure is complex. Compliance with regulatory frameworks like ISO/IEC 27001 and NIST adds another layer of difficulty. This chapter uses real-life examples to explore various IDPS architectures and detection mechanisms optimized for IMoT environments. It discusses future research directions, including federated learning for threat intelligence and quantum-resistant security models. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Hemant Kumar Saini, Sita Rani, Vikram Puri and Noor Zaman Jhanjhi individual chapters, the contributors.
