TI  - Securing LoRaWAN in the AIoT Era: A Systematic Mapping Study and an MITRE-Based Threat Matrix
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Internet of Things Journal
VL  - 13
IS  - 3
SP  - 3827
EP  - 3849
DO  - 10.1109/JIOT.2025.3633940
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105022434579?origin=resultslist
AD  - UniversitΓ© du QuΓ©bec Γ΅ Chicoutimi, Saguenay, G7H 2B1, QC, Canada
AD  - CΓ©gep de Sept-Γles, Sept-Γles, G4R 5B7, QC, Canada
AB  - The rapid expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT) has established long-range wide-area network (LoRaWAN) as a leading low-power, long-range communication protocol across critical domains such as smart cities, agriculture, and healthcare. However, its minimalist design and reliance on unlicensed spectrum expose vulnerabilities across the entire protocol stack from physical-layer jamming to medium access control (MAC)-layer spoofing and application-layer firmware attacks. Concurrently, the rise of the Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) introduces opportunities to reinforce LoRaWAN security via decentralized, intelligent, and adaptive mechanisms. This article presents a systematic mapping study of 81 peer-reviewed publications (2020β€“2025), conducted using a Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA)-based methodology. Our objectives are to: 1) identify key trends and research directions in LoRaWAN security; 2) propose an MITRE ATT&CK-inspired taxonomy tailored to the LoRaWAN stack; 3) analyze AIoT-based security contributions; and 4) highlight unresolved challenges and future perspectives. Our findings indicate that 62% of documented cyberattacks target the MAC layer, exploiting vulnerabilities such as static keys and weak integrity checks. AI-driven techniques, including radio frequency fingerprinting (RFF) [97% accuracy using convolutional neural networks (CNNs)], federated learning for anomaly detection, and blockchain-based key managementβ€”show promise but raise concerns about scalability and deployment on constrained devices. We introduce the first MITRE ATT&CK-LoRaWAN matrix, detailing 18 attack techniques [e.g., energy depletion and rogue gateways (GWs)] and associated countermeasures, including postquantum Kyber-1024 encryption. Finally, we discuss major technical, methodological, and interoperability challenges and suggest actionable research directions toward secure, AI-native, and resilient LoRaWAN infrastructures. Β© 2014 IEEE.
