AD  - Department of Computer Engineering, Sejong University, 209, Neungdong-ro, Gwangjin-gu, Seoul, 05006, South Korea
AB  - The growing penetration of renewable energy, electric vehicles, and distributed resources in South Koreaβ€™s electricity grid has introduced significant volatility, security risks, and coordination challenges. This paper presents a unified framework for adaptive electricity pricing that integrates quantum-enhanced forecasting, federated multi-agent coordination, and post-quantum-secure blockchain. Electricity price forecasts are generated using quantum kernel ridge regression (QKRR) with a Pauliβ€“ZZ feature map, providing competitive accuracy compared with optimized classical baselines. These forecasts guide a federated multi-agent reinforcement learning approach with proximal policy optimization (PPO), enabling privacy-preserving demandβ€“response and coordination under uncertainty. Market transactions are recorded on a Hyperledger Fabric ledger secured with Falcon lattice-based signatures, ensuring resilience against quantum-era attacks. The framework is validated on the Korean Electric Power Corporation (KEPCO), aligned distribution scenarios using a hybrid TensorFlow Federatedβ€“PennyLane simulation. Results show a normalized forecasting error of 0.081, a 17.0% increase in average daily profit compared to the baseline, and a 53% reduction in grid imbalance. Transaction latency under Raft ordering averages 68 ms, remaining well within the β‰¤100 ms performance class defined by IEC 61850β€“5 for fast automation and demandβ€“response applications. The proposed architecture demonstrates how quantum-resilient, federated, and blockchain-integrated solutions can enhance the stability, profitability, and security of South Koreaβ€™s smart grid. Β© 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
