AB  - Computer security faces constant challenges due to technological advancements and emerging threats. Traditional cryptographic algorithms, such as Rivestβ€“Shamirβ€“Adleman and elliptic curve cryptography, are being tested by quantum computing. Quantum-resistant cryptography aims to develop algorithms that can withstand attacks from both classical and quantum computers. Quantum key distribution (QKD) is a promising technology that uses quantum mechanics principles to establish secure cryptographic keys, enabling communication channels resistant to eavesdropping. QKD systems have potential applications in secure communication networks and financial transactions. One significant threat posed by quantum computing is the ability to break current encryption standards, making data vulnerable to attack. Quantum computers could also be used to develop sophisticated cyberattacks, such as targeted attacks against critical infrastructure or data theft. The chapter emphasizes the importance of ongoing research and development in postquantum cryptography, which focuses on identifying and developing new cryptographic algorithms capable of withstanding attacks from quantum computers. Β© 2026 Apple Academic Press, Inc. Co-published with CRC Press (Taylor & Francis)
