AB  - The quantum computing introduction causes risk for the security of Electronic Health Records (EHRs) by compromising RSA and ECC using Shor's and Grover's algorithms. To overcome for this type of risk we provide a Quantum-secure Lattice-Based EHR Management (QLBEM) system with combination of blockchain technology with NIST-recommended lattice cryptography (Dilithium for digital signatures and Kyber for key encapsulation) and the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS). The system practical performance could be protected with provision of guaranty for post-quantum confidentiality, integrity, and regulatory compliance. Comparing QLBEM to traditional RSA/ECC-based frameworks, experimental evaluation achieves throughput 16.5% greater than traditional algorithms, 21.4% of reduction in gas consumption, and lowers latency by 18.7%. The post-quantum implementation along with blockchain results in safeguard of healthcare could be demonstrated by the lattice primitives usage, which causes against quantum adversariesβ€™ prohibitive overhead. Through compliance with healthcare regulations (HIPAA, GDPR, DISHA) and NIST PQC standards (FIPS 203β€“205), QLBEM provides a proper future-proof architecture for safe, decentralized EHR management. A secure, scalable, and legally valid basis for EHR exchange across healthcare ecosystems is established by the suggested framework. The resultant of throughput, latency, quantum resistance level, security strength and memory overhead compared with traditional algorithms demonstrated in this paper. QLBEM model provides persistent confidentiality, integrity, and interoperability of healthcare data by hybrid technique with guaranty for post quantum security. Β© Authors.
