TI  - Quantum-Resilient GNN and CLD-Net Framework for Intrusion Detection in 6G Networks: Comparative Analysis with CNN, RNN, and Hybrid Deep Learning Models
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 10th International Conference on Science Technology, Engineering and Mathematics, ICONSTEM 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICONSTEM65670.2025.11374765
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034470621?origin=resultslist
AD  - Saveetha School of Engineering, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha University, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, 602105, India
AB  - The evolution of 6G networks introduces unprecedented bandwidth, ultra-low latency, and hyperconnectivity, creating both opportunities and vulnerabilities in network security. Conventional intrusion detection systems (IDS) based on a set of static signatures or simplistic forms of learning no longer suffice to respond to emerging threats like zero-day attacks and adversarial manipulation. This paper suggests a QuantumResilient graph neural network (GNN) with a Contextual Long-term dependency network (CLD-Net) to detect intrusions in 6G systems. The method uses Quantum Fourier Transform (QFT) and Quantum Bit Rotation (QBR) to encode resilient features, facilitating the encoding of spatiotemporal correlations unreachable in classical representations. Graph Convolutional Networks (GCN) with Gated Graph Sequence Networks (GGSN) model relations between flows, whereas Bi-LSTM and dilated convolution modules in the dual-pathway CLDNet model are useful in extracting multiscale time dependencies. A fusion layer that uses attention summarizes these insights to enable accurate classification. Lattice-based post-quantum cryptography secures model training, over a federated blockchainenabled infrastructure. Simulated 6G traffic experimental assessment of the CIC-DDoS dataset shows that the proposed model attains accuracy of between 90.14 and 90.91, precision of 90.27, recall of 90.12 and F1-score of 90.19, compared to the baseline CNN, RNN, and hybrid models. The findings affirm the capability of the suggested architecture to detect rogue and synchronized assaults in real time with a low latency. This study identifies the practicality of a quantum-resilient IDS in the next-generation networks that is capable of providing high detection rates, secure training, and strong generalization to new threats, thus delivering a scalable and future-resilient defense mechanism to critical 6 G infrastructures. Β© 2025 IEEE.
