AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) is revolutionizing sectors from healthcare to smart cities and from agriculture to manufacturing by connecting and allowing devices to capture data and make decisions. IoT and its 75-plus billion devices projected by 2025, is leading the charge for efficiency and data-informed decision making. Yet, there are still barriers to overcome; from scalability, security, energy efficiency, to interoperability. To address scalability challenges, local data processing facilitated by edge computing and fog computing, which can reduce network traffic. Security is addressed by using blockchain and quantum-safe encryption to increase data protection. There is an increasing enhancement of energy efficiency, achieved through the use of energy-harvesting sensors and low-power communication protocols in order to avoid the use of standard power supplies. Interoperability is being considered through standard communication protocols and general frameworks which allow virtual add-ons to the system. Continued advances in AI, decentralized systems, and quantum computing, hold the potential of further advancing what IoT can do. Partnership between industry, academia, and governments is vital for developing secure, scalable, and viable IoT ecosystems. The paper also emphasizes that addressing the challenges will be pivotal for IoT to contribute towards farreaching technological and industrial development.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
