AB  - The potential for Internet of Things (IoT) and sensor networks based devices to revolutionize dataβ€‘driven infrastructures in smart cities, healthcare, and industry is growing exponentially. However, as IoT and other emerging technologies expand in size and complexity, they also introduce issues related to data integrity, security, authentication, and trust management. This chapter provides an outline of how blockchain technology can serve as the foundation for securing IoT and sensor ecosystems. It includes a discussion of architectural design, implementation strategies, and case studies covering three main areas of application: healthcare monitoring, industrial automation, and smart grid systems. Performance evaluations demonstrate how blockchainβ€‘ enhanced IoT networks minimize the challenge of data tampering, unauthorized access, and denialβ€‘ofβ€‘service attacks, while also improving confidentiality, auditability, resilience, energy efficiency, and latency. Future directions include research on quantumβ€‘safe blockchain, AIβ€‘driven adaptive security, and federated interoperability across heterogeneous IoT platforms. By bridging IoT and blockchain technologies, it is possible to create added value by enabling the design of secure, transparent, and scalable infrastructures to operate in an increasingly digitized world. Β© 2026
