TI  - Encryption of Quantum-Resistant Algorithms for Cybersecurity: Enhancement of Post-Quantum Communication and Financial Systems
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - IEEE 10th International Conference on Smart Structures and Systems, ICSSS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICSSS66939.2025.11346099
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033147948?origin=resultslist
AD  - Site Reliability Engineer, UBS Business Solutions US LLC, 303 Sunderland dr, Mount Juliet, 37122, TN, United States
AD  - Koneru Lakshmaiah Education Foundation (K L Demeed to Be University), Department of CSE, Green Fields, Vaddeswaram, Andhra Pradesh, Guntur, 522302, India
AB  - As quantum computing technologies advance rapidly, the foundational cryptographic algorithms currently securing global digital infrastructure are at imminent risk of obsolescence. Conventional encryption methods such as RSA, ECC, and Diffie-Hellman are particularly vulnerable to quantum attacks, notably through Shor's algorithm, which can efficiently factor large integers and compute discrete logarithms. This emerging threat has accelerated the urgency for the development and implementation of quantum-resistant encryption algorithms, also referred to as post-quantum cryptography (PQC). This research focuses on the strategic deployment and optimization of quantum-resistant algorithms to enhance cybersecurity resilience in post-quantum communication and financial systems. The study examines a variety of quantum-resistant cryptographic schemes, including lattice-based, hash-based, code-based, multivariate polynomial, and isogeny-based algorithms. Through rigorous comparative analysis, the research evaluates these algorithms across multiple dimensions such as encryption/decryption speed, key and ciphertext sizes, computational overhead, integration complexity, and resistance to both quantum and classical attacks. The findings highlight lattice-based schemes like CRYSTALS-Kyber and CRYSTALS-Dilithium as promising candidates due to their balance between strong security guarantees and operational efficiency in real-world applications. A critical aspect of the study involves simulating the deployment of quantum-resistant encryption in communication protocols such as TLS/SSL and financial transaction platforms including blockchain-based ledgers, digital payment gateways, and banking APIs. The research assesses not only the cryptographic robustness but also the practical implications on latency, interoperability, and scalability within existing digital ecosystems. Moreover, the study explores hybrid encryption models that combine classical and quantum-resistant techniques as a transitional strategy to ensure backward compatibility and seamless migration during the post-quantum cryptographic shift. This work also delves into regulatory, standardization, and policy implications by evaluating the alignment of proposed algorithms with guidelines from global bodies such as NIST, ETSI, and ISO/IEC. Additionally, the research considers the economic and infrastructural impact on stakeholders, including governments, financial institutions, cloud service providers, and end-users, highlighting the necessity of early adoption and strategic preparedness.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
