AB  - he high rate of the spread of Internet of Things (IoT) equipment has added to pressure on the privacy ready and weightless of correspondence structures. Standard cryptographic schemes like RSA, ECC, are computationally intensive and susceptible to attacks using quantum computers, thus they cannot be applicable in the limited IoT setting. This study will introduce a new family of light-weight blockchain protocols combined with lattice-based cryptography that can guarantee quantum secure and energy-efficient data transmission. This protocol uses Ring-LWE based encryption (e.g. Kyber) and post-quantum digital signatures (e.g. Dilithium), with encryption times of at least 1.9 ms and decryption times of 2.0 ms giving a substantial advantage over RSA-2048 and ECC. A hybrid consensus that grafts Proof of Authentication and PBFT, can achieve a high throughput of 120 TPS on small-scale IoT network and maintains 78 TPS with 200 nodes as opposed to 36 TPS in traditional blockchain systems. Per-cryptographic operation energy consumption is minimised to 4.2 mJ; this is much lower than RSA at 12.3 mJ, so it is particularly well suited to low power architectures. Also, the average network latency is reduced to 112 ms and packet delivery ratio is 96 percent, making the proposed architecture reliable and time bound in nature. Altogether, the framework provides an effective post-quantum, scalable, and decentralized solution that best fits the modern IoT infrastructures. Β© 2025 IEEE.
