AB  - As the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) rapidly develops, it has greatly improved how smart grids operate, connect, and deliver energy. At the same time, more advanced cyber threats can now target critical infrastructure, due to this progress. Conventional information technology (IT) security approaches often encounter difficulties in protecting operational technology (OT) and communication systems in smart grids from ransomware, zero-day exploits, and attacks involving artificial intelligence. To address these weaknesses, this chapter examines using quantum-enhanced cybersecurity frameworks for IIoT-enabled power grids by combining Zero Trust Architecture, postquantum cryptography, and quantum machine learning. A Hybrid Quantum Convolutional Neural Network is presented for real-time anomaly detection, using quantum kernel methods to handle large volumes of OT/IT data. In addition, a quantum-safe blockchain method is introduced to confirm device identity and federated quantum learning is used for sharing cyber threats among grid devices. The framework is confirmed to work better than classical deep learning models in identifying ransomware, false data injection, and adversarial machine learning attacks, as shown by the latest Quantum-IIoT Intrusion Dataset. By using Quantum Key Distribution, lattice-based cryptography, and adaptive reinforcement learning, the method guarantees that the smart grid remains secure and able to recover on its own in the postquantum era. This chapter introduces new methods, studies, and unresolved challenges that help researchers and practitioners deploy quantum-safe cybersecurity for future IIoT power grids. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
