TI  - Enhancing Blockchain Security with Multivariate Post-Quantum Digital Signatures
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Intelligent Innovations in Engineering and Technology, ICIIET 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICIIET65921.2025.11377810
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035491423?origin=resultslist
AD  - Psg College of Technology, Department of Information Technology, TN, Coimbatore, India
AB  - Blockchain technology, recognized for its decentralized, immutable, and transparent characteristics, traditionally relies on elliptic curve cryptography (ECC) to secure digital signatures and ensure transaction integrity. However, the emergence of quantum computing poses a fundamental threat to ECC, as algorithms such as Shor's can efficiently solve the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem (ECDLP), rendering ECC-based blockchains insecure. This looming vulnerability necessitates the transition to post-quantum cryptographic (PQC) solutions. Among the various PQC families lattice-based, code-based, hash-based, and isogeny-based multivariate cryptography has emerged as the most suitable for digital signatures in blockchain applications, owing to its strong reliance on the hardness of the Multivariate Quadratic (MQ) problem, which remains intractable for both classical and quantum algorithms. Multivariate schemes provide several advantages, including fast signature generation, efficient verification, and scalability, all of which are essential for high-throughput blockchain networks. Within this family, the Unbalanced Oil and Vinegar (UOV) scheme stands out as the most efficient variant. Efficiency analysis shows that UOV achieves a 99.9% smaller private key than SPHINCS+ and 98.9% smaller than Dilithium 5, a 92.3% reduction in signature size compared to Dilithium 5, and a 99.6% lighter runtime memory requirement than GeMSS, enabling practical deployment even on resource-constrained blockchain nodes. Despite its slightly larger public key than Dilithium, UOV remains significantly more balanced than competing schemes, offering the optimal trade-off between quantum security, storage efficiency, and performance. This research explores the integration of multivariate digital signatures into blockchain, with a focus on UOV, to provide a quantum-resistant framework that ensures long-term transaction security, network scalability, and resilience in the post-quantum era.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
