AD  - The Center of Excellence Aicoms, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia
AD  - School of Electrical Engineering, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia
AB  - Quantum computing's rapid progress threatens traditional cryptography like Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (ECDSA) in blockchains. This paper examines CRYSTALS-Dilithium, a NIST-approved lattice-based signature scheme using Module Learning With Errors (MLWE) and Module Short Integer Solution (MSIS), for quantum-resistant blockchain security. However, Dilithium's complex key structure risks full compromise from partial leaks. We propose a three-layer defense: (1) Threshold Signature Scheme (TSS) distributes s1 across 20 nodes with threshold t=15 using Multi-Party Computation (MPC) signing to avoid single points of failure; (2) Dynamic Key Rotation (DKR) enables rapid revocation of s2 upon anomaly detection; (3) Verifiable Timed Signatures (VTS) leverage Timelock Puzzles (TLP) and Non-Interactive Zero-Knowledge Proofs (NIZKP) to impose timed delays, allowing detection of malicious activity. This architecture enhances post-quantum blockchain resilience, scalability, and decentralization while significantly increasing the cost of attacks. In 20-node simulations, baseline architectures fail after 4-5 node compromises, whereas our system endures up to 14, raising attack costs by a factor of 100. The combination of VTS and DKR reduces detection latency by 94.9% (from over 5 minutes to under 30 seconds). The overhead is approximately 2.5 KB per signature plus MPC operations, remaining feasible at 500 TPS for Proof-of-Work (PoW) or 100 TPS for Proof-of-Stake (PoS) systems. Β© 2025 IEEE.
